#uuid "79a0909f-afba-56f6-a5a0-082403b04be3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518154}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Logarithm of a Fraction or Root"} :step/id #uuid "7475992c-5ffa-5745-9e1e-8dc546465351" :step/math-academy-id 14005 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064067 :question/id #uuid "d137aa5c-f148-5a6f-bbda-0d88557f8ea9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3987"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1c6931-cc04-5014-9bf6-5ebc5dc07728" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518191}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Logarithms Using Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "c7faad3f-25a6-5d96-89ca-9640b6346ec0" :step/math-academy-id 14022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065355 :tutorial/content "We can evaluate some logarithm expressions by using the fact that the logarithm reverses the action of the exponent.\n\nThe idea is that the log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nFor example, in the case of $\\log_2 \\left( 2^{-1/2}\\right),$ we have $b=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac 1 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_2\\left(2^{-1/2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3299e585-1c90-521e-8207-847f81227d56" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2865 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "555e3d58-f4f5-5c7c-8a56-80938dcac0c2" :step/math-academy-id 15298 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065030}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065356 :tutorial/content "When the argument of the logarithm isn't written as an exponential, we can often still evaluate the logarithm. All we need to do is rewrite the argument so that it is in the form $b^x,$ where $b$ is the base of the logarithm.\n\nFor instance, to evaluate $\\log_2\\left( 8\\right),$ we rewrite $8$ as a power of $2$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n8 = 2^{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}{8} = \\log_{2}\\left(2^{3}\\right) .\n$$\n\nNow we can proceed as before. The log of a power of the base equals the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log_b(b^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b = 2$ and $x = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(2^{3}\\right) = 3.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1a5e22c2-205f-5cd6-bf98-c67977b99b73" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2866 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Logarithms Using Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "52e5cb38-4eef-573d-ba63-00b902ef60bd" :step/math-academy-id 15299 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065357 :tutorial/content "Recall that two special cases of exponentials are\n\n$$\nn^0 =1 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad n^1 =n.\n$$\n\nWhat do these look like in logarithm form? We can use the definition of a logarithm to find out:\n\n$a = {n}^{x}$ is equivalent to $\\log_{n}{a} = {x}.$\n\nUsing the above, we have that $n^0 =1$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\log_n 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nAlso, we have that $n^1 =n$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\log_n n = 1.\n$$\n\nThese are two special values for logarithms that are true for any base $n>0, n \\neq 1.$\n\n**Watch out!** We should always assume that the base $n$ of the logarithm does not equal $1.$ Recall that $\\log_n{a} = x$ is equivalent to\n$a = n^x.$ If $n=1$ that transforms to \n\n$$\na = 1^x.\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two possibilities:\n\n- if $a \\neq 1,$ we get that $a \\neq 1^x$ for any real $x,$ and\n\n- if $a = 1,$ we obtain that $1 = 1^x$ for all real $x.$\n\nIn both cases, the notation $\\log_1{a}$ does not make much sense." :tutorial/id #uuid "e668e2ae-df5f-5d1e-919a-80f3b07c0b72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2867 :tutorial/title "Special Values of Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "5f6eb5ac-5827-5b3b-96ac-7f951bbe5b1f" :step/math-academy-id 15300 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065031}}] :activity/title "Evaluating Logarithms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064462}]}
